{
	"name": "Fighting Style",
	"type": "feature",
	"img": "icons/skills/ranged/bullet-sparks-yellow.webp",
	"system": {
		"description": "<p>One of the first things a gunslinger needs to learn is how to fight with their firearms. Most choose a specific style that gives them a particular, and very dramatic, appearance on the battlefield. Often these styles inform the identity the gunslinger seeks to create for themself. Choose one of the following options:</p><p><strong>Akimbo:</strong> You treat light firearms as if they have the dual-wielding trait.</p><p><strong>Bullseye:</strong> You may expend your bonus action when you miss with a firearm to reroll the attack, but you must take the bullseye roll, even if it is worse.</p><p><strong>Saddleshooter:</strong> You gain expertise to avoid being unseated from a mount, and your mount gains your proficiency bonus to its combat maneuver DC.</p><p><strong>Sharpshooter:</strong> You ignore half or three quarters cover when using firearms. In addition, your range increments increase by 20ft with any firearm you wield.</p><p><strong>Shotgunner:</strong> Whenever you have advantage or disadvantage to an attack roll with a shotgun, you add 1d4 to the damage.</p><p><strong>Sword and Shot:</strong> When wielding a melee weapon in one hand, you do not gain disadvantage to ranged attacks while threatened.</p><p><strong>Trickshooter:</strong> You may use the Disarm, Knockdown, and Shove basic maneuvers as ranged attacks using a firearm. When you do so, you deal basic ranged damage equal to your Dexterity modifier instead of basic melee damage.</p>",
